  "title": "Upper Chambers at Kalhu",
  "provenance": "Pleiades",
  "placeTypeURIs": [
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/palace"
  ],
  "details": "<p>The Upper Chambers were excavated by <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austen_Henry_Layard\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Austen Henry Layard</a> in 1847\u201351 and <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Loftus\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">William Kennett Loftus</a> in 1854. The area consisted of a large hall and a suite of three small chambers. Its walls have fresco decoration depicting winged bulls, crenellated battlements, and geometric patterns.\u00a0Julian Reade (<a href=\"https://www.ucl.ac.uk/sargon/downloads/reade_mesopotamia_46_2011.pdf\">2011</a>: 117) has suggested that the Upper Chambers, which would have formed part of the <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"../309437155\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">palace of the Assyrian king Adad-n\u0101r\u0101r\u012b III</a> (r. 810-783 BC), \"would have been an appropriate residence for <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shammuramat\">Sammu-ramat</a>, Adad-nirari\u2019s mother, who clearly had great power during at least the early years of her son\u2019s reign. It would be an example of a palace built specifically\u00a0for a member of the royal family.\" J. Oates (<a class=\"external-link\" href=\"https://www.zotero.org/groups/2533/pleiades/search/Nimrud/titleCreatorYear/items/S55JVIUS/item-list\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">2004</a>: 70) has suggested that the Upper Chambers would have been \"a\u00a0prestigious and\u00a0attractive suite, on an upper floor and\u00a0almost certainly with a fine view of the Tigris valley.\" The walls excavated by Layard in the mid-nineteenth century\u00a0have now totally eroded.</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "This ornately-decorated, elevated building might have been the residence of Sammu-ramat (the legendary Semiramis) in the palace of her son Adad-n\u0101r\u0101r\u012b III (r. 810-783 BC) at Kalhu (biblical Calah, modern Nimrud). Because these rooms were built noticeably higher than the surrounding area, its first excavator Austen Henry Layard believed that he had uncovered part of an upper floor of an Assyrian palace and, therefore, referred to this area as the \u201cUpper Chambers.\u201d",
